<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management for the Bobcat source tree (src/). One pinned version per
    package so the Bobcat.* projects can never drift apart. The Critter Stack set is the
    canonical, mutually-compatible alignment, and samples/Directory.Packages.props must hold the
    SAME numbers — a sample project-references Bobcat, so it resolves this JasperFx.Events
    whatever its own store says:

      WolverineFx 6.46.0  ↔  Marten 9.46.0  ↔  Fisher 1.19.0  ↔  Polecat 5.36.0
      └─ all four BUILT against JasperFx(.Events) 2.80.2
      JasperFx(.Events) is PINNED AT 2.81.0 — one minor AHEAD of them, deliberately. Read
      "The 2.81.0 exception" below before touching it.

    ⚠️ READ THIS BEFORE MOVING JasperFx(.Events) ON ITS OWN. It was tried, on 2026-09-09, and
    it does not work. The reasoning was that every store declares JasperFx(.Events) only as a
    FLOOR (>= 2.56.0), so raising that one package satisfies them all and preserves the property
    that matters — every package resolving the SAME JasperFx.Events, so the event types unify.
    The resolution is indeed fine. The RUNTIME is not:

      System.TypeLoadException: Method 'QueryStreamStates' in type
      'Marten.Events.QueryEventStore' from assembly 'Marten, Version=9.30.0.0' does not have
      an implementation.
        at Marten.Internal.Sessions.QuerySession.CreateEventStore(...)

    JasperFx.Events 2.67 added `QueryStreamStates` to `IReadOnlyEventStore` as an ABSTRACT
    member. A store compiled against 2.56.0 has no slot for it, so the type fails to load the
    first time anything opens a session — which for Bobcat is `EventStores.fetchStreamAsync`,
    i.e. every arrange step. It took out Marten 9.30.0 and Fisher 1.0.4 identically: 13
    failures in Bobcat.CritterStack.Tests, 3 in Bobcat.Marten.Tests, 9 in
    Bobcat.CodeFirst.Samples, and the BankAccountES Fisher leg in the samples build.

    The general rule this leaves behind: **a floor constrains resolution, not the vtable.**
    `>= 2.56.0` says the store can RUN against 2.67.1 only for members that existed when it was
    compiled. An interface that gains an abstract member is a runtime break for every
    implementer, and no amount of nuspec reading predicts it — the only safe signal is that the
    store itself was BUILT against the JasperFx you are pinning. The set above satisfies that:
    Marten 9.46.0, Fisher 1.19.0, Polecat 5.36.0 and WolverineFx 6.46.0 each declare
    JasperFx(.Events) at EXACTLY 2.80.2, the pin, rather than merely at or below it, so each was
    built against the runtime it will load.

    ╔═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    ║ THE 2.81.0 EXCEPTION — the rule above still stands; this is a reasoned exception to it.
    ╚═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    **JasperFx and JasperFx.Events are pinned at 2.81.0 while every store is built against
    2.80.2.** That breaks the rule stated immediately above. **Decided by Jeremy on 2026-10-06**
    ("let's move to the latest JasperFx.Events now anyway — I think that's low risk"), for
    jasperfx#955's first train: issue #405 cannot be written at all without it. Checked in the
    nupkgs rather than inferred — `SourceWriterDirective` (jasperfx#956, the SourceWriter fixes
    #405 must write its output through) and `DomainPolicyBuilder` / `EventModelDomains` /
    `InChapter` / `StartsStream` (jasperfx#957, the fluent API that declares roles) are **absent
    from 2.80.2 and present in 2.81.0**; `git tag` reports both commits (b5a0eae, 2bfa916) as contained
    in V2.81.0 alone. 2.80.2's `EventModelBuilder` offers only `InDomain`/`Hotspot`/`Slice` and
    cannot state a pattern, a chapter or any role, which is the whole output #405 specifies.

    WHY THIS IS SAFE, AND WHAT THAT ARGUMENT DOES NOT COVER — the asymmetry is the point:

    1. The hazard the rule above is NAMED for is the 2.67-class `TypeLoadException`: an existing
       interface gaining an ABSTRACT member, which no store compiled earlier has a slot for. For
       this bump that is **positively excluded, not merely untripped.** jasperfx#933/#937 added a
       ratchet — `src/EventStoreTests/ContractGrowth/abstract-interface-members.txt`, the record of
       every abstract member as of the last release — built for exactly this failure, and it is
       **byte-identical between V2.80.1 and V2.81.0** (md5 c3e45382b62d9b6843a3c6caba1a76fe, 809
       lines, both). Upstream's policy now is that a member added to an existing interface in a
       minor carries a default implementation.

    2. Measured on the real stores, not reasoned about. On this pin: `CI=true ./build.sh CI` green,
       with `Bobcat.CritterStack.Tests` 41/41 against real Marten on Postgres 5445 and real Fisher
       and nothing skipped; `./build.sh Samples` green across CompileSamples / TestSamples /
       TestSamplesOnFisher, including BankAccountES on BOTH legs (Marten 20/20, Fisher 20/20). The
       2026-09 failure surfaced as a TypeLoadException on the first session open, which every
       arrange step hits, so these suites genuinely detect it.

    3. **The residual hazard the ratchet does NOT cover, and this half is weaker.** It is the
       mirror-image failure the block below describes: a new member in an enum-backed capability
       set (`EventQuery.TagValues` joining `EventQueryFilters.All` is the example) makes a store
       CLAIM a capability it does not implement and **return wrong results silently** rather than
       throwing. Enum constants inline at compile time, so a store built against 2.80.2 cannot
       claim a 2.81.0 addition — which is the same way being old protected the stores in 2026-09.
       No such addition was found in the 2.80.1..2.81.0 diff, which is confined to the EventModeling
       descriptors, the fluent builders and SourceWriter.

       **"No such addition was found" is an argument from absence, and it is strictly weaker than
       the ratchet's positive guarantee in (1).** A future reader needs that distinction more than
       the conclusion: if something starts returning too much rather than throwing, this is the
       paragraph that was the weak link — not (1), and not (2), since neither an exception-shaped
       break nor a wrong-results break on a path the suites exercise would have survived them.

    WHEN THIS EXCEPTION ENDS: as soon as Marten / Fisher / Polecat / WolverineFx ship builds that
    declare JasperFx(.Events) 2.81.0 or later, move the stores onto them and delete this block —
    the exception exists only to bridge that gap, and leaving it standing past its reason is how a
    one-off becomes a habit.

    Why JasperFx had to move to 2.81.0: jasperfx#955's first train. Issue #405 writes the emlang
    import out as C# — stub records plus one `EventModelDefinition` — and that needs two things
    that only exist from 2.81.0: the fluent event-model API that declares roles by string or by
    type (jasperfx#957) and `SourceWriter`'s whitespace fixes, which #405 is required to write
    its output through (jasperfx#956). The stores moved to 2.80.2's generation to keep the set
    coherent underneath it — as close as a released store gets — and the one-minor gap that leaves
    is the exception documented above. The earlier 2.67.1 jump was CritterWatch#1212 — the Event Model wire needs
    partial descriptors from several producers to round-trip, and slice constructor collections
    arriving null from System.Text.Json threw `ArgumentNullException` during serialization
    (jasperfx#807).

    ⚠️ The mirror-image hazard is still live and is why the set must move as a UNIT. On
    CritterWatch's own bump, `EventQuery.TagValues` joined `EventQueryFilters.All`: a store
    REBUILT against the newer JasperFx without implementing the filter would CLAIM it and
    silently return unfiltered results. Enum constants are inlined at compile time, so the old
    stores were safe from that one precisely because they were old — the exact property this
    bump gives up. Now that the stores ARE rebuilt against 2.67, a filter they claim is a filter
    they must honour; that is upstream's contract to keep, but a silent wrong-results failure is
    the shape to look for if event queries start returning too much.

    Re-read from the packages' own nuspecs on 2026-10-06, because this block had gone stale
    against the pins below once before and a stale "canonical set" note is exactly the hazard it
    exists to prevent:
      WolverineFx 6.46.0        requires JasperFx and JasperFx.Events >= 2.80.2
      WolverineFx.Marten 6.46.0 requires Marten >= 9.46.0, Weasel.Storage >= 9.41.0
      WolverineFx.Fisher 6.46.0 requires Fisher >= 1.19.0, Weasel.Storage >= 9.41.0
      Marten 9.46.0             requires JasperFx(.Events) >= 2.80.2, Weasel.Postgresql >= 9.41.0
      Fisher 1.19.0             requires JasperFx(.Events) >= 2.80.2, Weasel.Sqlite >= 9.41.0
      Polecat 5.36.0            requires JasperFx(.Events) >= 2.80.2, Weasel.SqlServer >= 9.41.0

    Every floor is 2.80.2 and the pin is 2.81.0, so the pin is ABOVE every floor rather than
    equal to it — see the exception above for why that is deliberate here. What still holds, and
    is the property that actually matters, is that every package resolves ONE JasperFx.Events, so
    the event types (IEvent, etc.) unify. Confirmed in the resolved graph rather than inferred:
    Bobcat.CritterStack.Tests references Marten AND Fisher and restores a single JasperFx.Events
    2.81.0, with Weasel.Storage unifying at 9.41.0.

    One consequence of Marten + Fisher together: both nupkgs bundle the IDENTICAL
    JasperFx.Events.SourceGenerator (byte-for-byte), so a project referencing both stores loads
    the generator twice and every projection's Evolver partial is emitted twice (CS0433/CS0111 —
    jasperfx#462, the same trap CritterWatch documents). Fix, ported from CritterWatch: drop ALL
    store-bundled copies via a DropDuplicateBundledEventSourceGenerator target and compile with
    the explicit JasperFx.Events.SourceGenerator pinned below, so the generator always matches
    the JasperFx.Events runtime whatever the stores happen to bundle.

    On THIS set all three stores' bundled copies are byte-identical (Marten 9.46.0, Fisher 1.19.0
    and Polecat 5.36.0 each ship SHA-256 3b9551b7…), so they dedupe themselves and the target is
    currently belt-and-braces. That is the trap, not the all-clear: a project without the target
    compiles fine today and starts failing CS0433 the moment a bump puts two stores on different
    JasperFx builds.

    JasperFx.Events.EventModeling is what issues #106/#107/#108 implement against; the semantic
    model (SpecificationDescriptor, HotspotDescriptor.PendingSpecification, SlicePattern,
    EventModelDiscovery, the element/lane/palette model) arrived in 2.54.0 via
    JasperFx/jasperfx#687 and #689, and has been reshaped since. A consuming sample pinned below
    2.54.0 cannot see it at all.

    See versions.md for the full matrix and the samples' target set.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Critter Stack — mutually-compatible aligned set -->
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx" Version="6.46.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.RuntimeCompilation" Version="6.46.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.Marten" Version="6.46.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.Fisher" Version="6.46.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Marten" Version="9.46.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx" Version="2.81.0" />
    <!--
      Bobcat.CritterStack binds to the JasperFx.Events abstractions (IEventStore, IEventDatabase,
      IProjectionCoordinator, ProjectionScenario) rather than to Marten, so one package serves
      Marten, Polecat and Fisher. Same version as JasperFx — the two ship together.

      Fisher (SQLite, the inner-loop store) and Polecat (SQL Server) are pinned here so that the
      moment a project references them it lands on this JasperFx and no other. Fisher coverage in
      Bobcat.CritterStack.Tests (a temp SQLite file, no docker) and the BankAccountES-on-Fisher
      acceptance are issue #103's follow-on to this bump; Polecat needs SQL Server and is a
      documented manual path (CLAUDE.md, "Bobcat.CritterStack is store-agnostic").
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx.Events" Version="2.81.0" />
    <!-- Referenced explicitly (as an analyzer) by any project referencing MORE than one store,
         with the store-bundled copies dropped — see the dual-store note in the header. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="JasperFx.Events.SourceGenerator" Version="2.81.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Fisher" Version="1.19.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Polecat" Version="5.36.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Alba" Version="8.5.2" />
    <!-- Bobcat.Tests only. AlbaContentRoot reads [WebApplicationFactoryContentRoot] reflectively
         by type name so the shipped package needs no ASP.NET reference; the test assembly carries
         the real attribute so the reflective read is proved against the real type. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.0" />

    <!-- Entity Framework Core (Bobcat.EntityFrameworkCore) -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" Version="10.0.0" />

    <!--
      Wolverine host packages. Same 6.46.0 as the aligned set above — the earlier monitor-only
      version split existed only while the rest of the repo was still on Wolverine 6.5.1, and
      collapsed when the alignment caught up.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.Http" Version="6.46.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.SignalR" Version="6.46.0" />
    <!-- Test-only: the real broker issue #282's transport-isolation tests need. No Bobcat.* library
         references it — the reset seam is transport-agnostic; only the proof is RabbitMQ-shaped. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="WolverineFx.RabbitMQ" Version="6.46.0" />
        <!--
      TypeScript contract codegen for the monitor's Vue SPA (CritterWatch's GenerateCommand
      pattern): NJsonSchema reflects the MonitorEvents.cs records through the same STJ settings
      the wire uses, so the .ts mirrors can only ever say what the serializer does.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="NJsonSchema" Version="11.5.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NJsonSchema.CodeGeneration.TypeScript" Version="11.5.2" />
    <!--
      Consumed by Bobcat.EventModel: the curated YAML event-model loader and the emlang importer
      (#201/#202), which the `bobcat` CLI exposes as import-event-model.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="YamlDotNet" Version="16.3.0" />

    <!--
      Microsoft.Testing.Platform (Bobcat.Mtp — Bobcat as an MTP test host).

      Pinned to 1.9.1 to match what xunit.v3 3.2.2 builds against (its package is literally named
      xunit.v3.core.mtp-v1). Forcing 2.x here loads a platform assembly whose types have moved,
      and xUnit's auto-registered MSBuild extension dies with a TypeLoadException on
      IDataConsumer. Aligning on the version the xUnit v3 ecosystem uses is also what lets a
      consumer mix Bobcat specs and xUnit v3 tests in one solution — the point of issue #41.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Testing.Platform" Version="1.9.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Testing.Platform.MSBuild" Version="1.9.1" />

    <!-- Roslyn (source generator only) -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" Version="4.12.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers" Version="3.11.0" />

    <!--
      Test stack. xUnit v3 ships as a Microsoft.Testing.Platform test host, so the test projects
      are self-executing MTP applications rather than VSTest assemblies. That means no
      Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, no xunit.runner.visualstudio, and no coverlet.collector (a VSTest
      data collector) — MTP supplies its own equivalents.

      This also lets Bobcat's own specs run beside these projects under one runner, which is the
      point of the MTP work in issue #41.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
    <!--
      Runner adapters (Bobcat.Xunit, Bobcat.TUnit — issue #110). Each takes the smallest piece of
      its runner that an adapter can be written against: xunit.v3.extensibility.core is what
      xunit.v3 itself layers the runner and assertions on top of, and TUnit.Core is TUnit's
      equivalent. A consumer already has the full package; an adapter that dragged it in would be
      pinning that consumer's runner version from underneath.

      xunit.v3.extensibility.core moves in lockstep with xunit.v3 above and must stay equal to it.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.v3.extensibility.core" Version="3.2.2" />
    <PackageVersion Include="TUnit.Core" Version="1.66.27" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />
    <!-- Only the F# shape fixtures for the partial-object tests (bobcat#417); nothing shipped references it. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="FSharp.Core" Version="9.0.303" />
    <PackageVersion Include="NSubstitute" Version="5.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
